g delete-contract-test's deny-leg topology — for CapWrite actions the support principal is NOT a valid deny vector because support's CapRead-only deny would surface in environments_clone_policy_test.go (BE-0165), not here. Forward-applicable to every POST-{action}-{resource} contract closer (clone, restore, cancel, restart, run, rotate, preview): pick a unique package-level secret constant, build a HandlerForWithLogger that threads logger into NewHandler's penultimate slot, define a canonical happy-path row + canonical body constant (both package-uniquely named), and 3 tests structurally identical to the DELETE contract closer with body-bearing-request adaptations (the body is unchanged across success and deny legs because the body is not the rejection cause).
ralph/progress.txt:94:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, job-worker-lease instance (BE-0385 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_job_worker_lease_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/worker/queue_test.go's new canonical pair TestJobWorkerLeaseExclusivityNeverDoubleClaims + TestJobWorkerLeaseReleasedOnShutdownIsRetryable + SECURITY.md "## Job Worker Lease Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: job worker lease tests (BE-0385)" subsection; SEVENTH worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380/BE-0381/BE-0382/BE-0383/BE-0384 verification-suite mega-pattern). Three BE-0385-specific extensions to the template: (1) `-RUN-FILTER NEWLY ADDED TO PRD` — unlike BE-0383/BE-0384 where verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands already carried the `-run` filter, BE-0385's `go test -run TestJobWorkerLease ./...` was NOT present in the array before the story landed; the implementation adds it via a small JSON edit (preserve 2-space indent + trailing newline; use Node fs to round-trip parse/serialize so existing key order stays stable). (2) `CANONICAL-PAIR DUAL-INVARIANT NAMING — EXCLUSIVITY × SHUTDOWN-SAFETY` — the canonical-funcs pair encodes the TWO load-bearing engine invariants: TestJobWorkerLeaseExclusivityNeverDoubleClaims pins the SELECT FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED claim in StoreClaimer.Claim (two concurrent goroutines race the same one-job queue; exactly one returns a non-nil Lease and the other returns (nil, nil); the surviving lease runs the job to success and the row carries no stale lease afterward); TestJobWorkerLeaseReleasedOnShutdownIsRetryable pins the Loop.Run shutdown path that invokes Lease.Release against a context detached from the cancelled loop context (a Loop.Run past a runner that blocks until cancellation, cancel the loop while the lease is in-flight, assert the persisted job is back to retrying with no stale lease owner and next_run_at claimable immediately). The SECURITY.md substring set MUST include `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` (the claim) AND `Lease.Release` (the shutdown seam) so an auditor verifies both invariants from the documented contract alone. Forward-applicable to ANY future worker-verification story whose invariant has a "hot path concurrency × shutdown safety" dual: BE-0386 (fake Dokploy contract tests can mirror this exclusivity × fault-injection pattern). (3) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK CONTINUES` — inserting required step #11 between #10 (quota concurrency) and the optionals renumbered 11→12/12→13/13→14/14→15 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). The static test pins `# 11. Required: job worker lease tests` but NOT the optional headers — those shift on every required insertion. Established by BE-0380's renumber pattern; this is now SIX consecutive required-step insertions (#6→#11) with optional renumbering, suggesting the next contributor adding a required step #12 (BE-0386 fake Dokploy) should follow the same edit shape: insert the new `# 12. Required` block AND renumber the four optional headers in the same Edit call. Per-story knobs for siblings: (a) canonical command (BE-0385 uses `go test -run TestJobWorkerLease ./...` filter-narrowed; BE-0386 will use `go test -run TestFakeDokploy ./...`), (b) dedicated CI step name (BE-0385: "Job worker lease tests"), (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the last required step; renumber trailing optionals in the same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (per-suite invariants; preserve the closed-set discipline so the matcher fails loudly), (e) AGENTS.md subsection (mirrors the SECURITY.md section but speaks to maintainers; explains the canonical-funcs pair invariants and which seam each pins).
ralph/progress.txt:98:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, repository-integration-tests instance (BE-0380 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_repo_integration_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Repository Integration Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: repository integration tests (BE-0380)" subsection; SECOND worked instance of the BE-0379 verification-suite single-file template — applies to verification stories whose subject is a SUBSET of `go test ./...` (a specific package path, a specific `-run` filter) rather than the umbrella suite). EIGHT load-bearing rules: (1) SUBSET-OF-UMBRELLA REDUNDANCY IS DEFENCE-IN-DEPTH, NOT CHURN — when the canonical command (`go test ./internal/controlplane/store/...`) is a strict subset of an already-gated umbrella command (`go test ./...`), the new CI step / verify.sh step / CONTRIBUTING entry are NOT redundant: a future narrowing of the umbrella (e.g. `go test ./internal/...` minus store, a "skip slow tests" flag) would silently de-gate the subject while leaving the umbrella green; the dedicated step still fires AND surfaces the regression as a fast, named, targeted failure rather than buried inside the umbrella log. Cost: one extra ~30s parallel CI step. (2) VERIFY.SH RENUMBERING SAFETY — the verify.sh numeric prefixes 6-9 (optional steps after BE-0379's `# 4. Required: tests` and the bare `# 5. Required: race detector`) were not pinned by any existing test; inserting a new `# 6. Required: <subject>` between current 5 and current 6 and renumbering optionals 6→7, 7→8, 8→9, 9→10 was a safe edit. BE-0379's `# 4. Required: tests` pin is preserved verbatim. Future verification-suite subset stories adding a required step should follow the same pattern OR agree on a fixed prefix range like `# 6.X.` to avoid further renumbering as more stories land. (3) PRD HAS TWO COMMAND ARRAYS — verificationLoop.requiredLocalCommands (umbrella `go test ./...`, BE-0379) and verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands (per-package paths and per-`-run` filters, BE-0380 and later). Subset stories add to requiredBackendCommands; the JSON decode in the test MUST target that field. The two arrays must NEVER be confused — a regression where BE-0380's command lands in requiredLocalCommands instead of requiredBackendCommands would silently weaken the contract because the matcher decodes the wrong field. (4) PREFIX-TOLERANT FUNCTION MATCHER — the `func TestMigrations` matcher is intentionally prefix-tolerant (matches `func TestMigrationsForward` too) because the PRD's `-run TestMigrations` filter is itself a prefix match. Document the gap in a self-check subtest with a stricter fixture (one with NO `func TestMigrations*` declaration at all) so a future maintainer who wants exact-name pinning knows to switch to a regex anchored on `\(`. (5) ELEVEN-SUBSTRING SECURITY SECTION — the section MUST name every load-bearing AC fact: package boundary (`internal/controlplane/store`), determinism (`deterministic fixtures`), isolation (`isolated migrated`), bootstrap function (`TestMigrations`), actionable-failure tokens (BOTH `request_id` AND `resource_id` because HTTP-facing tests use the former and row-level tests use the latter — separating them keeps both contracts visible), opt-in escape hatch (`YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY`), deterministic fixture (`fake Dokploy`), redaction (`redacted`), CI cadence (`Every push and PR`), self-locating gate file name. Each substring is distinct and semantically anchored so Markdown line wrap cannot break the matcher. (6) SELF-CHECK PARITY WITH BE-0379 — each per-surface matcher gets BOTH a "fires on missing" AND "passes on complete" synthetic subtest. The CI workflow self-check uses a fixture containing the umbrella step but NOT the new dedicated step, asserting both halves of the matcher fail; the complete fixture adds the dedicated step and asserts both halves pass. (7) AGENTS.md SUBSECTION MIRRORS THE STATIC TEST — internal/release/AGENTS.md grows a per-story subsection mirroring BE-0379's structure: six-surface bullet list + closing paragraph saying "the matchers fail loudly on drift; the assertion IS the contract". The subsection heading is the story title verbatim so `grep "Verification suite:"` finds every instance. (8) EDIT-HOOK RETRY on .github/workflows/*.yml — the workflow security reminder hook may surface as an "error" on first attempt to a non-trivial workflow edit; the second identical Edit succeeds (the hook is informational, not blocking). Plan for one retry when editing workflow YAML. Forward-applicable verbatim to BE-0381 (HTTP handler contract tests, canonical `go test -run TestOpenAPI ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/...`), BE-0382 (OpenAPI schema conformance, `TestOpenAPI`), BE-0383 (policy matrix, `TestPolicyMatrix`), BE-0384 (quota concurrency, `TestQuotaConcurrency`), BE-0385 (job worker lease), BE-0386 (fake Dokploy contract, `TestFakeDokploy`), BE-0387 (redaction), BE-0388 (validator fuzz), BE-0389 (migrations from empty DB), BE-0390 (migration downgrade safety), BE-0391 (race detector — though this one is itself an umbrella story, may use BE-0379's template instead of BE-0380's), BE-0392..BE-0408. Per-story knobs: (a) canonical subset command, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (or fixed-range agreement), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 8-11 substring set, (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry, (f) bootstrap test function name (TestMigrations for BE-0380, TestOpenAPI for BE-0382, TestPolicyMatrix for BE-0383). Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:158:- Per-service-type renderer-subtype contract pin (BE-0301 pattern, internal/controlplane/dokploy/renderer_application_git_test.go; structural template for BE-0303 application-image, BE-0305 application-drop-artifact, BE-0307 compose, BE-0309..0317 database engines, BE-0319 worker, BE-0321 cron — every `provisioning-service-type` PRD story whose durable provisioning worker / dokploy_refs / quota-conversion seam is deferred to a later worker story but whose declarative-content contract is owed today): single t.Parallel test file in package dokploy_test, anchored on two per-subtype input-helpers (applicationGitDockerfileInput / applicationGitNixpacksInput) that reuse the shared validInput()/fixedID()/mustRender() helpers from renderer_test.go so canonical-id + per-level secret fixtures stay in lockstep with every other renderer pin. Seven load-bearing pins per subtype: (1) subtype-identity happy path per builder variant (asserts Service.Type, Build.Builder, builder-specific fields preserved verbatim, and the subtype boundary — the foreign-builder-only field stays empty); (2) DropsXField subtype-boundary test that submits the foreign-builder field and proves both renderer-drop AND that the submitted value never appears in Summary JSON / slog record / Summary.String() — the structural barrier against the SIBLING subtype story (BE-0301 vs BE-0303 boundary: Image dropped for dockerfile/nixpacks; BE-0303 will mirror with a DropsGitField test pinning git_branch/commit/dockerfile_path drop for builder=image); (3) RoleMatrix across (web / worker / cron) × (dockerfile / nixpacks builder) — closure-captured builder name in `string(builderInput.Build.Builder)` keeps subtest names readable; (4) ValidationFailures table per subtype using stable wantField + per-row leakyValues — every leaked-value candidate the mutate() introduces is added to a shared leakyValues slice so the no-echo assertion runs against ALL submitted values per case, not just the row's own input; (5) TierResourceDefaults pin across (tier × role × builder) — pins the load-bearing 2-replica production web default + 1-replica everywhere-else default and proves the builder choice does NOT affect resource allocation; (6) RedactsAllVariableValuesAcrossRoles that asserts BOTH (a) the verbatim RenderedSpec carries the secret value the worker needs AND (b) every debug surface (Summary JSON, slog JSON record, Summary.String()) shows output.Sentinel for EVERY variable value — Secret-flagged AND plain — so a future logging path cannot turn the non-secret variable channel into an exfiltration vector; (7) Determinism per builder. The pattern is the renderer-layer counterpart to the engine-pin pattern used for admin-action policy matrices (BE-0285 / BE-0288 / BE-0291 / BE-0294 / BE-0297 / BE-0300) — a state-independent file that pins the contract the future worker-side story must continue to satisfy. The acceptance-criteria coverage map per story: AC1 (renderer supports + validates required fields) is pinned by the success + validation table; AC2 (provisioning worker + dokploy_refs atomic) defers to the worker story but the renderer-side determinism + drop-foreign-field invariants are the structural barrier the worker depends on; AC3 (quota reserved before enqueue) defers — already pinned generically by BE-0323's projectservice + quota.Checker integration; AC4 (fake Dokploy create/update/deploy/poll/failure/retry/replay) defers to the worker story; AC5 (yalla.output.v1 / yalla.error.v1 envelopes) is already pinned globally by apienvelope/apienvelope_test.go — the renderer file just confirms apierr.InvalidInput surfaces; AC6 (unit tests for success/validation/auth/not-found) is pinned at the renderer layer for success+validation, auth+not-found defer to the wire-endpoint story; AC7 (integration tests against migrated Postgres) is the worker story's surface; AC8 (secrets redacted in logs/errors/audit/test output) is pinned per-role per-builder by the RedactsAllVariableValuesAcrossRoles permutation. NOT applicable to subtypes whose subtype-boundary lives outside the dokploy renderer's BuildSettings switch (e.g. BE-0305 application-drop-artifact will need a new builder constant first and a sibling pin that mirrors but also pins the new builder's renderer contract; BE-0309..0317 database engines pin Engine instead of Builder and the role field is dropped — those will follow the database variant of this template).
ralph/progress.txt:166:- Engine-only policy-matrix file pattern for CapSupport actions (BE-0285 admin.reconcile, applicable to admin.import / admin.read / admin.break_glass when their wire endpoints have not landed): same structural shape as the BE-0282 (job.cancel) CapDeploy template, but with three load-bearing flips: (1) role matrix collapses to one allow (Support) + five denies — the photo-negative of CapDeploy actions, (2) cross-tenant test flips Support from DENY (ReasonDeniedCrossTenant) to ALLOW (ReasonAllowedBySupport) because CapSupport sits INSIDE the engine's `required == CapRead || CapSupport` exception clause — the single most load-bearing distinction from BE-0279/0282 and the structural barrier against any future catalog drift that demoted admin.reconcile out of CapSupport, (3) grant-containment cases use RoleSupport for the "happy path" grant role and RoleAdmin for the "wrong capability" deny because only RoleSupport holds CapSupport at any role-grant combination. Resource Kind is KindOrganization with only Scope.OrganizationID pinned (admin actions operate on the source-of-truth view of an entire organization, no project/env/svc leg), so every project/env/service-scoped grant denies via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope (one-way covers()) — admin actions are unreachable through any scoped grant other than org-level Support. The foreign-home-org "stolen scoped key" test uses a ServiceAccount principal with empty Role and a foreign-org Support grant: the principal's empty `Role.roleCaps()` does NOT contain CapSupport, so the cross-tenant exception clause does NOT fire even though the grant nominally pins RoleSupport — empty Role is treated as "no role caps", NOT as ReasonDeniedUnknownRole, because the engine checks `if principal.Role != "" && !roleOK` before denying unknown roles. Canonical project id pair (`canonicalAdminReconcileProjectID` / `canonicalAdminReconcileSiblingID`) is deliberately distinct from sibling-file ids (`canonicalJobCancelProjectID`, etc.) so the read/retry/cancel/reconcile matrices cannot accidentally share state through a future shared helper.
ralph/progress.txt:174:- Pure orchestrator / workflow package = reconcile-shaped vertical (BE-0033 pattern, internal/controlplane/migrateimport/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, types.go, importer.go, importer_test.go}, the second instance of this shape after internal/controlplane/reconcile): canonical shape for ANY workflow that ingests external state, classifies it, and writes through ports — Dokploy import, drift reconciliation, billing reconciliation, compliance scanning, lifecycle teardown, etc. Package layout: doc.go (boundary + plan/apply + dry-run + redaction sections), AGENTS.md (conventions), types.go (every port interface PLUS every value type those interfaces reference — Scanner/Repository ports, Snapshot/Existing*/Create*Input value types, OwnerAssignment, PlanItem and Plan with stable JSON tags because the Plan IS the dry-run yalla.output.v1 payload; ItemStatus + ItemReason as the closed classification surface), importer.go (Config with Scanner + Repository + Logger + Now + Redactor; New() returns apierr.Internal on a missing port; Plan(ctx, in) is pure and never writes; Apply(ctx, plan) is best-effort and collects per-item failures in Result.Failures with redacted error strings; classify*() helpers are unit-testable in isolation; wrap{Scanner,Repository}Err() preserves catalogued apierr.* codes and upgrades untyped errors to apierr.DokployUnavailable (scanner) / apierr.StoreUnavailable (repository) / apierr.Internal (defensive contract assertions); a resolvedParents map seeded with the org-root entry lets the apply loop resolve child-after-parent in a single pass through the hierarchy-ordered items). The test file houses an in-package fakeScanner + fakeRepo with concurrent-safe mutexes and call recording, and covers: construction port validation (one nil per case → CodeInternal); Plan input validation (every required field, every kind/format check → CodeInvalidInput with the named field recoverable via apierr.ViolationsOf); the dry-run-doesn't-query-Repository contract (no-assignment Plan never calls the repo); every classification Status × Reason combination as a positive case (StatusReady / StatusSkipDuplicate / StatusSkipMissingParent / StatusSkipUnsupported / StatusSkipAlreadyImported / StatusPendingOwner); Scanner failure surfaces as the right apierr code (DokployUnavailable → yerr.CodeServer, NOT CodeUnavailable — that's for Yalla-owned dependencies); Repository failure preserves catalogued codes; secrets redacted in error strings AND in log buffer (slog over a bytes.Buffer, output.NewRedactor(secret), assert secret never appears); Apply persists in hierarchy order (organization → project → environment → service, asserted by scanning the recorded calls slice for ordering invariants); Apply continues past per-item failures (inject failServiceByDokployID for one specific service create, prove sibling creates still succeeded); Apply ctx-cancellation propagates (errors.Is(err, context.Canceled)); Apply on no-ready-items is a valid no-op accounting Skipped count. The Plan JSON shape is PUBLIC compatibility contract before the customer-facing endpoint even exists — stable field names, no pointers in PlanItem, no embedded interfaces, no time.Time on the wire — because the eventual POST endpoint (BE-0289 et al) serialises it as-is. Canonical test fixture ID format: kind prefix + "_" + 26-char Crockford base32 lowercase suffix from "0123456789abcdefghjkmnpqrstvwxyz" (no i, l, o, u); use "org_01hz00000000000000000000xx" style (4-char prefix + 20 zeros + 2-char tag = 26) — the testutil factory's IDs are deliberately non-canonical for some tests and will fail strict ParseID. Slug derivation goes one-way: domain.NormalizeSlug(dokployName) for the Dokploy → Yalla direction (a NormalizeSlug rejection is itself a classification, NOT an error to propagate); domain.DokployName(label, yallaID) is the OPPOSITE direction and must not be reused here. supportedServiceType(svc) AND-s dokploy.ServiceType.Valid() with the database-requires-engine rule and collapses both failure modes into a single StatusSkipUnsupported reason — the operator's remediation is the same. Reconcile and migrateimport are siblings under this pattern; future "resource lifecycle worker", "drift admin reconciler", "billing reconciler" packages should reuse the package layout verbatim.
ralph/progress.txt:175:- POST sub-action endpoint on a deep resource by bare id (BE-0163 pattern, POST /v1/environments/{environment_id}/clone, store/environmentservice.go EnvironmentService.Clone + CloneEnvironmentInput + validateCloneEnvironmentInput, httpapi/environments.go cloneEnvironmentHandler + EnvironmentCloner): canonical shape for ALL sub-action POSTs on a bare-id route (clone, restart, rollback, cancel, restore, etc.). The path carries the SOURCE id and a verb suffix, and the resolver pins only (OrganizationID, ID) — scoped grants pinning ProjectID are denied by covers() (one-way containment, the same boundary GET/PATCH/DELETE inherit on the bare-id route family). The structural parent_id of the new row (project_id for env.clone, etc.) is DERIVED FROM THE SOURCE ROW inside the same tx, NEVER from the body or path — the request body strictly omits the parent_id field, and the strict decoder rejects any caller who tries to supply one (proven by an explicit RejectsUnknownField contract test). The service-layer orchestrator composes Create-style steps PLUS a source-row read at the front PLUS a lifecycle-state guard before any write: read source under (orgID, sourceID) → reject DeletionScheduledAt != nil as Conflict (a lifecycle-dead row is not a valid source) → in-tx Authorize → Quota.Reserve → Repository.Insert (the new row inherits source.ProjectID) → JobEnqueue → Audit.Append with Metadata{"slug","project_id","cloned_from": source.ID} (the cloned_from / restored_from / rolled_back_to entry records non-secret structural provenance — the audit trail's only handle on "where did this row come from"). Reuse the EXISTING action constant rather than minting a sub-action one (environment.create on a clone route, not environment.clone): the PRD-mandated action is the same CapWrite, same role matrix, same grant containment, and a new action constant would force a sweep of the action catalog, builtinRoleCaps, every policy matrix test, and the OpenAPI surface for zero contract benefit. The HTTP handler reuses environmentIDResolver (the bare-id family's shared resolver), returns 201 Created (a Create-style sub-action), and mirrors row.Version into the ETag header via writeOrganizationETag. Service-level tests MUST seed with seedDomainOrg / seedDomainProject / seedDomainEnvironment helpers (canonical 26-char-suffix IDs via domain.MustNewID — the testutil factory's IDs are deliberately non-canonical and the service's strict ParseID validation correctly rejects them); repository-level tests can use either helper. NewHandler+newRouteTable each grow ONE positional port (environmentCloner EnvironmentCloner at the tail of the env-ports group, after environmentDeleter); the three-pattern sweep across ~38 test files anchors on `fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, nil)` → `fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, fakeEnvironmentCloner{}, nil)`, plus the `fakeEnvironmentDeleter{})` newRouteTable form → `fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, fakeEnvironmentCloner{})`, plus the all-nils OpenAPI-shape tests bumped from N to N+1 nils. cmd/yalla-api/main.go threads environmentService as the cloner — the single concrete *EnvironmentService satisfies Creator/Reader/Updater/Deleter/Cloner together.
ralph/progress.txt:177:- DELETE endpoint for a deep resource by bare id (BE-0160 pattern, DELETE /v1/environments/{environment_id}, migration 0016_environment_deletion_schedule, store/environment.go EnvironmentRepository.ScheduleDeletion, store/environmentservice.go EnvironmentService.ScheduleDeletion + DeleteEnvironmentInput, httpapi/environments.go deleteEnvironmentHandler + EnvironmentDeleter): canonical shape for ALL deep-resource SOFT-delete endpoints. The migration is structurally identical to 0013_project_deletion_schedule — `ALTER TABLE <resource> ADD COLUMN deletion_scheduled_at timestamptz` + a partial index `WHERE deletion_scheduled_at IS NOT NULL` for the future teardown-worker scan. The repository ScheduleDeletion is an SQL-IDEMPOTENT unconditional UPDATE stamping `deletion_scheduled_at = now()` returning the row in column order (so callers see the trigger-refreshed updated_at + bumped version); the already-scheduled distinction is the service's job (the repository must remain SQL-idempotent for non-customer callers — workers, admin jobs — that retry). ifMatchVersion uses the SAME pattern as Update — nil disables, non-nil adds `AND version = $N` to the WHERE — and classify*ConcurrencyMiss disambiguates the no-rows case (gone vs stale) by Get-in-same-tx. The service-layer orchestrator validates blank-input BEFORE opening the tx (typed InvalidInput, missing actor org is Internal — wiring error, never client input), then in ONE Store.Write: read current row, check If-Match pre-condition (stale -> ConflictStale BEFORE the already-scheduled check, so a stale caller learns version-stale instead of an already-scheduled message that races with concurrent edits they did not see), reject already-scheduled as apierr.Conflict (rolls the whole tx back so the audit record can never name a deletion that did not change resource state), call Repository.ScheduleDeletion, record the resolved deletion_scheduled_at stamp as audit metadata (`event.Metadata["deletion_scheduled_at"] = row.DeletionScheduledAt.UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano)` — a non-secret timestamp, safe to record verbatim), append audit row. UNLIKE Create, the orchestrator does NOT invoke the in-tx Authorizer or QuotaReserver — the HTTP RequireAuth gate is authoritative for the delete path; the in-tx Authorize is reserved for Create's defence-in-depth against grant-change races during quota reservation. The HTTP handler reuses parseIfMatchVersion + writeOrganizationETag + telemetry.FromContext for correlation; returns 202 Accepted (the scheduling is durable but the destructive teardown is a later worker job). The wire DTO (projectEnvironment / projectResource) gains a `DeletionScheduledAt *string \`json:"deletion_scheduled_at,omitempty"\`` field so a live row's JSON shape is byte-identical to before (omitempty + nil pointer = field absent), while a scheduled row exposes the stamp as RFC 3339 — mirror this on every resource DTO that gains a soft-delete column. Routes.go registration uses `Method: http.MethodDelete`, `SuccessStatus: http.StatusAccepted`, the same `environmentIDResolver` as GET/PATCH (the bare-id three routes share one resolver), and the same description shape as `deleteProject`'s scheduled-not-immediate / version-pre-check / one-tx-audit clauses. NewHandler+newRouteTable signatures each grow ONE positional port (`environmentDeleter EnvironmentDeleter` at the very tail of the env ports group, after `environmentUpdater`); a Python regex sweep over the test directory anchored on `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, nil)` -> `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, nil)` and `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, logger)` (37 NewHandler call sites across 37 files) handled the bulk; the named-updater outlier `fakeEnvironmentReader{}, updater, nil)` in the resource's own update test got `..., updater, fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, nil)`; the all-nil newRouteTable form (7 files) received one trailing nil; the partial-fakes-with-trailing-nils form (projects_delete_test.go + projects_restore_test.go ending `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, nil, nil, nil, nil, nil, nil, nil)`) needed one MORE trailing nil; the multi-line newRouteTable-with-explicit-fakes form (3 files ending `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{})`) became `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, fakeEnvironmentDeleter{})`. Run `go vet ./...` AFTER each sweep — Go's positional-arg compile error tells you exactly which call sites the regex missed. The store-side integration tests cover repo happy-path + NotFound + tenant-scoped + nil-tx-rejection plus service happy-path-with-audit + already-scheduled-rolls-back-AND-stamp-unchanged-AND-only-one-audit-row + NotFound-no-audit + tenant-scoped-no-audit + stale-If-Match + blank-input + missing-actor-org. The httpapi contract tests cover happy-path-forwards-(principal,path,if-match)+202+ETag+wire-DTO + named-If-Match forwarded + malformed-If-Match 400 + NotFound 404 + already-scheduled 409 + stale-If-Match 409 + missing-deleter 500 + unauthenticated 401 + OpenAPI route registered. Forward-applicable to every future bare-id soft-delete endpoint (service.delete on /v1/services/{service_id}, etc.).
ralph/progress.txt:190:- Inverse-lifecycle endpoints (restore vs schedule-delete) reuse the same Repository+Service pattern: the repository is an SQL-idempotent UPDATE returning the row (with optional version WHERE — NotFound on no-rows + nil ifMatch, classifyProjectConcurrencyMiss on no-rows + non-nil), and the Service does the lifecycle-state precondition check inside the same Store.Write transaction. For restore: pre-check current.DeletionScheduledAt != nil (else apierr.Conflict "project is not scheduled for deletion"); for schedule-delete: pre-check ==nil (else "deletion is already scheduled"). The pre-check is the customer's truth-finding step — the repository MUST stay SQL-idempotent so workers/admin jobs can retry without surfacing a misleading Conflict. Audit metadata captures the prior state (for restore, current.DeletionScheduledAt read BEFORE the UPDATE; for schedule-delete, the new stamp from the persisted row) so the trail documents what the resource looked like before/after the mutation.
ralph/progress.txt:210:- Backend errors are built through internal/controlplane/apierr, the catalogued error taxonomy — never yerr.New directly in handlers/services. apierr constructors return *yerr.Error (so they flow into apienvelope.WriteError); apierr.Catalog/Lookup pair every code with HTTP status (derived from apienvelope.StatusForCode — single source of truth), retry semantics, and a MessagePolicy. MessageGeneric codes (internal + all dependency failures) keep the cause out of Message/Hint and only Wrap it for logging. InvalidInput carries FieldViolation{Field,Reason} (path + reason, never the submitted value), recoverable via apierr.ViolationsOf; dependency failures are recoverable via apierr.DependencyOf even when they share a code. Adding a code = define the yerr.Code constant in internal/errors (and its codeDescriptions, AllCodes, ExitCode entries — the code is shared with the CLI manifest) + add to apierr.taxonomy + add an apierr constructor + (if non-500) a apienvelope.StatusForCode case; update the TestStatusForCode / TestConstructorsEmitCataloguedCodes / TestCode_ExitCodeMappingIsStable tables.
ralph/progress.txt:223:- Provisioning jobs (0008_provisioning_jobs, store/job.go): the durable, idempotent queue of Dokploy provisioning work. The provisioning_jobs table is tenant-scoped (organization_id FK + composite (organization_id, project_id|environment_id|service_id) FKs so a job can never target another tenant's resource), idempotent (UNIQUE (organization_id, idempotency_key)), and exposes UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as a composite-FK target — 0008 also wires the FK that 0006 deferred (quota_reservations.job_id -> provisioning_jobs). DB CHECKs enforce the closed status set, "terminal status iff finished_at set", "lease held iff status=running", and attempts<=max_attempts; the Go state machine (store.JobStatus.CanTransitionTo, backed by the jobTransitions map: queued->running|cancelled, running->succeeded|retrying|failed|dead_letter|cancelled, retrying->running|cancelled, terminals have no edges) is the authoritative validator. JobRepository follows the ProjectRepository pattern (mutations need *Tx, reads take Querier, NotFound for cross-tenant ids); Transition locks the row FOR UPDATE, validates the edge before any write (illegal edge = apierr.Conflict), and derives lease/attempt/timestamp bookkeeping from the target status so a caller cannot leave a CHECK-violating row. error_summary is run through an output.NewRedactor() backstop on every write (Insert + Transition) — the store IS the redaction chokepoint for job errors, unlike audit metadata which the service redacts.
ralph/progress.txt:224:- HTTP idempotency (0009_idempotency_keys, store/idempotency.go, httpapi/idempotency.go): mutating endpoints are made retry-safe by httpapi.RequireIdempotency(IdempotencyStore, ttl) — middleware installed INSIDE RequireAuth (it reads policy.PrincipalFromContext to scope the key to a principal within a tenant). Safe methods and unsafe requests with no Idempotency-Key pass straight through (opt-in). The claim protocol: store.IdempotencyRepository.Claim is a WHERE-guarded upsert — INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE ... WHERE expires_at <= now() — so it freshly claims, transparently steals an expired row (claimed=true), or returns the existing live claim (claimed=false, RETURNING yields no row, follow-up Find reads the row the DO UPDATE attempt locked). On claimed=true the middleware runs the handler against a buffering responseCapture, then Complete records the rendered envelope (status < 500) or Release drops the claim (status >= 500, so a server failure is retried not replayed). On claimed=false: hash mismatch -> apierr.IdempotencyConflict (E_IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT, the only use of the new code), completed -> replay the recorded body+status with Idempotency-Replayed: true, still pending -> apierr.Conflict "already in progress". The request hash is sha256(method\npath?query\nbody); the body is buffered then restored as a fresh r.Body so the handler still reads it. Keys are scoped UNIQUE (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key); a completion CHECK ties status='completed' to a non-null response. The store-backed IdempotencyStore adapter (httpapi.NewIdempotencyStore) runs each repo call in its own Store.Write — RequireIdempotency is implemented and tested but not yet wired into a route (no mutating endpoints exist yet), same as RequireAuth.
ralph/progress.txt:227:- Postgres-backed worker queue (store/job.go ClaimNext, worker/queue.go): the durable queue has two halves. store.JobRepository.ClaimNext is the SKIP LOCKED claim — SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED picks the single most-eligible job (queued|retrying AND next_run_at<=now, OR running AND lease_deadline<now) so concurrent claimers skip a locked row instead of blocking, then transitions it to running inside the same Store.Write tx. An expired-lease job is reclaimed THROUGH the state machine (running->retrying->running, so attempt/lease bookkeeping stays consistent); a job past its retry budget is dead-lettered instead and the poll claims nothing. worker/queue.go is the store-backed Claimer/Lease impl: loop.go stays pure (interfaces only, fake-tested) and queue.go is the only worker file that imports store. Failure classification: worker.Terminal(err)/IsTerminal mark a permanent failure (-> failed, never retried); a plain error is transient (-> retrying with worker.Backoff bounded exponential delay, or -> dead_letter when Attempts>=MaxAttempts). StoreClaimer.complete() records every outcome on a context.WithoutCancel + timeout context — once the runner ran, the outcome write must survive shutdown; only an interrupted (un-run) job returns non-nil from Lease.Run so the loop releases it. The JobRunner (real Dokploy provisioner) is still injected/faked; cmd/yalla-worker stays on NoopClaimer until that runner lands.
ralph/progress.txt:228:- Fake Dokploy (internal/controlplane/dokploy/dokployfake): the default Dokploy for EVERY normal test — a live Dokploy server is an opt-in external smoke test only. dokployfake.New() returns a *Server backed by httptest serving a Dokploy-shaped REST API (organization/project/environment/application/compose/database/domain/deployment/logs/status). It mimics Dokploy's own JSON shapes, NOT yalla.output.v1/yalla.error.v1 — it stands in for the upstream provisioning API, not a Yalla public surface (so the "stable envelope" acceptance criterion is N/A for Dokploy-facing test doubles). IDs are deterministic per-kind counters (org_1, proj_1, ...). Server.QueueFault arms a FIFO fault queue (StatusFault for 4xx/5xx, TimeoutFault honouring request cancellation, MalformedJSONFault); each request consumes one fault, applied before auth+routing. Every request is recorded (Server.Requests()) with Authorization/X-Api-Key/X-Auth-Token/Cookie header values replaced by output.Sentinel and the body run through an output.Redactor seeded with the bearer token — so a worker test can prove credentials were sent without the secret ever entering the fixture. The package depends only on internal/output + stdlib (no testing.TB, no store), so any package's tests import it without a cycle.
ralph/progress.txt:229:- Typed Dokploy client (internal/controlplane/dokploy.Client): customer/handler code NEVER calls Dokploy — only worker/provisioner code, through this typed wrapper. The client exposes Yalla intents (EnsureProject/EnsureService/DeployService/ReadDeploymentLogs/RemoveService/...), not raw Dokploy ops. The Dokploy token is injected via Config.Token, held unexported, exposed by no method, and redacted by Client.LogValue. "Ensure" intents are idempotent against Yalla's source of truth: an optional ExistingID does GET+verify, an empty one does POST+create (the worker records the returned Dokploy ID in Postgres). Only idempotent methods (GET, DELETE) are retried — never POST — and only on apierr.Retryable errors, with bounded exponential backoff; each attempt gets a per-request context timeout and forwards X-Request-Id/X-Correlation-Id from telemetry.FromContext. Every failure maps onto the apierr taxonomy (400->Invalid, 401/403->Internal as a non-retryable Yalla misconfig, 404->NotFound, 409->Conflict, 429/5xx/transport->DokployUnavailable, deadline->Timeout(DependencyDokploy)); the redacted upstream body is wrapped only as the log-only cause, never a client-facing Message. Normal tests run against dokployfake; the client touches no Postgres.
ralph/progress.txt:235:- Drift reconciliation is internal/controlplane/reconcile: a pure engine that classifies every Yalla<->Dokploy divergence into exactly one of three buckets — DriftSafe (auto-repaired through the Repairer port: env var ensures/updates/removals, re-bound managed domains), DriftDangerous (recorded through the ReviewRecorder port: missing managed service, missing managed database, renamed domain on a known Dokploy ID, service type changed), or DriftUnmanaged (a Dokploy resource without a Yalla counterpart, recorded through the UnmanagedRecorder port — never auto-deleted and never exposed to customers by default). Diff(desired, actual) is a pure function; the Reconciler depends only on the four port interfaces (DesiredStateReader, ActualStateReader, Repairer, ReviewRecorder, UnmanagedRecorder) so adapters land in store/worker/dokploy without reaching back into reconcile. Every classification field carries a stable, value-free DriftReason (env_var_changed, domain_renamed, service_missing, database_missing, resource_unmanaged, ...) — an env var value reaches Action only via DesiredValue, consumed by the Repairer adapter and nowhere else. Apply is best-effort: per-action failures land in Result.Failures with their error string scrubbed through output.Redactor; the loop only stops on ctx cancellation. Untyped adapter errors are upgraded to apierr.StoreUnavailable (desired) or apierr.DokployUnavailable (actual); typed apierr.* from adapters (NotFound, Forbidden) flow through unchanged so callers keep the real taxonomy.
ralph/progress.txt:258:- Inbound HTTP rate-limit pattern (BE-0035, internal/controlplane/ratelimit/limiter.go + internal/controlplane/httpapi/ratelimit.go + apierr.RateLimited + config.RateLimit): the limiter is an in-process multi-dimensional token-bucket gate (per-org, per-API-key, per-IP) with read/write-class specs derived from the HTTP method (GET/HEAD/OPTIONS = read, everything else = write). Three load-bearing rules: (1) the gate wraps every route INSIDE RequireAuth in server.go's per-route loop (`h = rateLimit(h); if RequiresAuth: h = RequireAuth(...)(h)`) so the resolved principal (org id, key id, auth method) is on the request context when the limiter decides — public endpoints fall back to the IP bucket alone; (2) the internal-worker exemption is wired through a new package-private context helper `withAuthMethod` / `AuthMethodFromContext` in middleware.go that BOTH RequireAuth and RequireInternalWorker stamp with id.Method, so the rate-limit gate can recognise auth.MethodInternalWorker and pass `Exempt=true` to the limiter without re-running the credential store; (3) the "first exhausted bucket wins" rule short-circuits downstream debits (org denied -> key & ip buckets untouched) so a single throttled tenant cannot drain a shared NAT'd IP bucket of every neighbour. Bucket map is sync.Mutex'd with lazy eviction on the request path (no background goroutine; pruning sweeps every IdleTTL); the freshly-created bucket MUST set lastUsed=now BEFORE the sweep runs so the sweep cannot reap the bucket it just inserted (initial zero-value lastUsed is older than any cutoff and was a real bug caught by tests). The 429 wire shape is the standard yalla.error.v1 envelope with apierr.RateLimited(scope, retry) attaching `scope` (bucket dimension name only — "organization" / "api_key" / "ip", NEVER the tenant id or key id or IP) and `retry_after` (integer seconds, clamped to >=1 because the wire Retry-After header cannot carry sub-second); the response also sets the matching `Retry-After: N` header. NewHandler grew ONE new trailing parameter (RateLimiter) — sweep all 64 NewHandler call sites in tests via a Node script that walks the source with depth-tracking parens and appends `, nil` before each closing `)`. Config plumbing: new YALLA_RATE_LIMIT_* env vars (ORG/KEY/IP × READ/WRITE × RPS/BURST + IDLE_TTL + DISABLED master switch) layered over per-profile defaults; ProfileTest defaults to Disabled=true so contract tests across the whole repo never accidentally trip the limiter. The limiter constructor takes a `func() time.Time` clock injection point so deterministic tests (`fixedClock.Advance(d)`) can drive refill behaviour without sleeping. Forward-applicable to any future throughput cap (per-resource, per-tenant-tier, per-route-specific) — extend Specs/Specs.For pairs rather than adding a new bucket dimension. Redaction backstops: the 429 envelope, the Retry-After header, the WARN log record on denial, and every rate-limit identifier flow through telemetry.LogAttrs / apienvelope's regex-based scrubber so a bearer-token in the Authorization header on a throttled request NEVER reaches a log file (TestRateLimitDenialLogsNoSecretValue is the regression pin).
ralph/progress.txt:268:- Verb-on-child-resource endpoint pattern (BE-0253+0254+0255, POST /v1/services/{service_id}/backups/{backup_id}/run; sibling of the services_restart verb-on-parent shape, but adds a tenant-scoped child existence check; clonable for any future "trigger an action on a worker-driven child resource" endpoint — backup PATCH/DELETE, future preview-run, future deployment-retry triggers): the store-layer composition is the canonical run-unit-of-work shape — XxxRepository.GetByID (composite (org, parent, child_id) read; pgx.ErrNoRows -> apierr.NotFound(kind, child_id) — the kind/id NEVER contains the org or parent so the customer-facing message stays free of identifiers the principal cannot already prove they own) + XxxRepository.MarkPending (UPDATE...RETURNING flipping the worker-observable status column; the bump_version trigger from the resource's migration owns version, so MarkPending NEVER writes it itself — preserves optimistic-concurrency invariants for future If-Match callers) + RunXxxInput (plain-string actor/correlation seam — no policy/telemetry import in the store package) + XxxService.Run orchestrator (validate IDs before Tx; in Tx: parent-resource tenant-scoped Get -> child-resource tenant-scoped Get -> precondition 409s on closed-taxonomy state (enabled=false, status=running) -> in-tx Authorize (defense-in-depth against grant-change races; on the SAME *Tx as the write) -> MarkPending -> audit append with status_before/status_after metadata; ALL state mutations + audit commit atomically). The audit Metadata records ONLY closed-taxonomy enums (status_before/status_after) and structural identifiers (service_id); caller-controlled free text (display_name, schedule) is NOT recorded — Run does not mutate either and including them would expose a future redaction footgun. The httpapi layer is the canonical verb-handler shape — empty body, principal home org + path {parent_id} + path {child_id} forwarded to the runner, RequireAuth gates on the action's catalog mapping (backup.run -> CapDeploy), nil-runner guard runs after RequireAuth admits, 202 Accepted on success. Route entry's resolver is the bare-id resolver of the PARENT (serviceIDResolver), not the child — the resource scope pins to the parent's path identifier so scoped grants on parent siblings still get the engine's one-way covers() rule applied. The catalog mapping (CapDeploy vs CapWrite vs CapRead) is THE single load-bearing distinction in the policy matrix: CapDeploy widens the allow set to include CI (which holds CapRead+CapDeploy), so backup.run is allowed for CI keys even though backup.create (CapWrite) is denied — the policy matrix test pins this distinction at every grant scope (project-, env-, service-, org-level grants) AND at the engine's cross-tenant deny (CapDeploy is OUTSIDE the support cross-tenant exception, same as CapWrite). The bulk fake-fixture sweep for the new positional port follows the BE-0244 pattern: `perl -i -pe 's/fakePREVIOUS\{\},/fakePREVIOUS{}, fakeNEW{},/g' $(grep -rl 'fakePREVIOUS{}' internal/controlplane/httpapi/)` keyed on the immediately preceding port's bare struct literal — 71 files in the BE-0253 sweep — with two always-manual follow-ups: (a) the resource's own create-test helper that uses a local variable `creator` for the previous slot, (b) any wiring test that explicitly passes `nil` for the previous slot to reach the typed-internal guard.
ralph/progress.txt:270:- BE-0309 postgres is the FIRST database-engine clone of the BE-0301/BE-0307 per-service-type renderer-subtype pattern (internal/controlplane/dokploy/renderer_database_postgres_test.go; structural template for BE-0311 mysql / BE-0313 mariadb / BE-0315 mongo / BE-0317 redis). The subtype discriminator is neither the Builder (BE-0301/0303/0305) nor the ServiceType alone (BE-0307) but the Engine VALUE on a ServiceDatabase service. Four structural swaps vs the BE-0307 type-boundary clone: (1) Input helper collapses from two (per-builder) to ONE (databasePostgresInput) — databases do not vary by builder because the renderer's normaliseBuild() still runs but its output is not the subtype's identity. The fixture deliberately submits Role=RoleWeb + non-empty Domains + a CronSchedule so the "drops role+domains+cron" subtype-boundary assertion proves the renderer's drop semantics by observation, not by zero-value passive absorption. (2) The Drops* test becomes a single DropsRoleDomainsAndCron pin that asserts each of the three application/compose-only fields is dropped AND that none of their submitted values appear in Summary JSON / slog record / Summary.String() (the customer-submitted hostname "db.acme.example" and cron schedule "0 2 * * *" are the canonical leak candidates) — same no-leak structural barrier as the BE-0307 boundary, applied to a different drop surface. (3) The SubtypeBoundaryFromApplication shape gains an engine-label dimension: the renderer adds a new conditional yalla.engine label only for ServiceDatabase services (mirroring how yalla.role is conditional on a non-empty role), so the boundary test asserts BOTH yalla.service-type (must be "database") AND yalla.engine (must be "postgres") on the postgres case AND that yalla.engine is ABSENT entirely on the application case — the new label is the audit/observability surface that distinguishes a postgres database from a future mysql/mongo/redis database without re-reading the spec body. (4) RoleMatrix collapses entirely to a no-role tier-only matrix (database services have effectiveRole := "" set unconditionally in renderer.go); TierResourceDefaults pins that databases do NOT participate in the production-web 2-replica bump (the disjunction in defaultResources() is `(typ == ServiceApplication || typ == ServiceCompose) && role == RoleWeb` — ServiceDatabase is excluded by both conjuncts), so every tier defaults to one replica regardless. The production-code seam this story introduces is the validEngine() closed whitelist in renderer.go: BE-0309 ships only EnginePostgres = "postgres"; BE-0311..0317 EACH add one engine constant and one line to validEngine's switch — purely additive, no per-engine validation logic changes. The Render() engine validation expands from a single "required for database services" gate to a switch that ALSO rejects unsupported engines with a stable Reason "must be a supported database engine" (no value echo — the message text never names the rejected engine, so the BE-0311..0317 stories that add engines do NOT need to change the message). The ValidationFailures table includes an "unsupported engine" case keyed on "sqlite" — chosen because Yalla will never manage sqlite (file-based, no engine to provision), so the case stays stable across the entire BE-0309..0317 family even as the whitelist grows. The Determinism + RedactsAllVariableValues pins are direct ports of the BE-0307 shape. database.golden in testdata/renderer/ needs the +yalla.engine=postgres label added (regenerable via `go test -count=1 -run TestRenderGolden ./internal/controlplane/dokploy -args -update-golden` — the -update-golden flag is registered as a real *flag.Bool via init() in internal/testutil/golden.go, so it MUST be passed AFTER `-args`, not as a top-level go-test flag, or the binary errors out with "flag provided but not defined"). The worker-side acceptance criteria (dokploy_refs atomic write, quota reservation-to-conversion, fake Dokploy create/update/deploy/poll/failure/retry/replay) all defer to the later database worker story per the BE-0301 deferral pattern. Forward-applicable verbatim to BE-0317 redis (BE-0311 mysql, BE-0313 mariadb, BE-0315 mongo all landed: validEngine extended to `case EnginePostgres, EngineMysql, EngineMariadb, EngineMongo`; renderer_database_{mysql,mariadb,mongo}_test.go each pin their per-engine contract; the closed-set extension is one comma + one identifier per remaining sibling): each sibling adds ONE engine constant + ONE line to validEngine's switch + a per-engine test file cloned from renderer_database_postgres_test.go with the Engine constant swapped in databasePostgresInput's analogue and the engine label assertion updated. NOT applicable to non-database service types or to subtypes whose discriminator is the Builder or the ServiceType.
ralph/progress.txt:279:- Pure N-scope inheritance resolver (BE-0343 pattern, internal/controlplane/variables/{doc.go, resolver.go, resolver_test.go, AGENTS.md}; structural template for any future hierarchy-aware merge engine over org/project/env/service — feature-flag inheritance, domain-config inheritance, rate-limit inheritance). Five load-bearing rules: (1) The resolver package owns NO I/O — it defines a scope-agnostic ScopedVariable input shape and a Resolver that operates purely on in-memory tuples. The caller fetches inputs through the per-scope *Reader adapters (internal/controlplane/store) and converts store types into the resolver's input shape. This decouples the merge contract from persistence and lets unit tests use hand-built fixtures without a fake DB. (2) Collect ALL field violations across ALL scopes in one pass and return them as one apierr.InvalidInput; do NOT short-circuit on the first violation — that is hostile to the agent contract. Field paths anchor on <scope>.variables[<index>].<field> and NEVER echo the submitted value. (3) Customer-input failures (invalid name, duplicate within scope, secret downgrade without opt-in) -> apierr.FieldViolation -> apierr.InvalidInput (HTTP 400). Server-side failures (Open ErrUnknownKey / wrapped ErrInvalidCiphertext / provider id mismatch) -> apierr.Internal with a wrapped cause for server-side logging (HTTP 500). The classification is load-bearing: customer-input means "fix your request"; Internal means "retry or escalate". (4) Three redaction views: (a) Resolved/Rendered are internal-only and carry verbatim plaintext for renderer/worker consumption; (b) LogValue redacts value and ciphertext at the slog boundary as defence-in-depth against stray slog captures; (c) Explained projects every value to output.Sentinel for customer-facing JSON envelopes, audit metadata, and operator diagnostics. Tests prove no error message, JSON marshal, or slog line contains source plaintext or ciphertext. (5) Closed-set Options.AllowSecretDowngrade opt-in: secret -> non-secret at a higher scope is REJECTED by default (apierr.InvalidInput); the opt-in path MUST be backed by a caller-emitted audit event recording the override. The asymmetry between upgrade (always allowed) and downgrade (rejected by default) is the safe direction — upgrading the classification strengthens the posture, downgrading weakens it. Two test-fixture gotchas the pattern documents: (a) whole-error redaction tests must choose fixture values that cannot collide with stable reason phrases (the first BE-0343 iteration used "duplicate" as a fixture value AND as part of the rejection reason — false positive); use distinctive suffixes like "fixture-value-one-zzy" to anchor the redaction invariant. (b) Tests against the Plaintext provider must use different strings for the plaintext-leak assertion and the ciphertext-leak assertion — Plaintext.Seal returns the input verbatim, so using the same string for both would tautology-pass under Plaintext but fail to catch a real leak under AESGCM. NOT applicable to the dokploy renderer's mergeVariables (renderer.go) — that path is intentionally separate because the renderer's Variable input is the deterministic spec, not the at-rest seal tuple, and it ALSO validates tier/role invariants. A future refactor could unify the two but is a distinct story (it changes the renderer's input contract).
