ralph/progress.txt:19581:- Remaining risks / follow-ups: BE-0277 (Implement POST /v1/jobs/{job_id}/retry) and BE-0278 (contract tests) still pending — the engine pin here is the authoritative contract until they land. The wire 404-vs-403 distinction for cross-tenant or unknown {job_id} (treat foreign-tenant or unknown-row {job_id} as not-found for non-Support roles, and ALSO not-found for Support because mutating cross-tenant must go through admin break-glass tooling, not the customer-facing retry verb) lives in the contract test for BE-0278 and is intentionally not pinned at the engine layer. Similarly, the job-state precondition (cannot retry a row already in a terminal succeeded state, can retry from failed/cancelled per the worker's idempotency contract) is a service-layer guard, not a policy-engine guard, and is pinned in the BE-0277 service-level tests when they land.
ralph/progress.txt:19583:  - Bare-{id} CapDeploy verb engine pins (job.retry BE-0279, future job.cancel BE-0282, future deployment-by-id mutate verbs like deployment.cancel-by-id / deployment.rollback-by-id at /v1/deployments/{deployment_id}/cancel / .../rollback) follow the EXACT SAME containment shape as the bare-{id} CapRead pin (job_read_byid BE-0276) with three mechanical differences. (1) The role matrix flips from six-row allow (CapRead) to four-row allow / two-row deny (CapDeploy) — Viewer and Support deny ReasonDeniedNoCapability in the role matrix and in the org-grant capability matrix. (2) The cross-tenant matrix flips Support from allow (ReasonAllowedBySupport via the CapRead+CapSupport exception) to deny (ReasonDeniedCrossTenant — CapDeploy is OUTSIDE the exception). (3) Project/env/service grant fixtures use RoleAdmin (or RoleCI for the explicit CI pin) instead of RoleViewer because Viewer doesn't hold CapDeploy and would deny on capability rather than scope — RoleAdmin keeps the test ANCHORED on scope containment, with one explicit "viewer on target project denies no capability" subtest in the project-grant suite to anchor on capability instead. Pattern locks in for BE-0282 (job.cancel) which is the IDENTICAL CapDeploy + bare-{id} story for the cancel verb — mechanical clone of this file by s/Retry/Cancel/ + s/retry/cancel/ + s/ActionJobRetry/ActionJobCancel/.
ralph/progress.txt:19584:  - The "wire endpoint pending" pattern reaches 5 stories now: preview_create (BE-0264), preview_read (BE-0267), preview_delete (BE-0270), job_read (BE-0273), job_read_byid (BE-0276), job_retry (BE-0279). The next obvious clone in the bare-{id} CapDeploy family is BE-0282 (job.cancel — POST /v1/jobs/{job_id}/cancel) which mechanically clones this file; after that, the BE-0285/0288/0291 admin-dokploy P=1 trio (drift/reconcile/import) which need a DIFFERENT structural template because admin endpoints use ActionAdminRead/ActionAdminReconcile/ActionAdminImport (CapSupport — Support-only, every other role denied) and a Yalla-internal-admin-org scope shape, not a customer-organization scope shape. The admin variant is a fresh structural template, not a clone of any existing file in the package.
ralph/progress.txt:19585:  - The "stolen scoped key" pin (TestJobRetryPolicyForeignHomeOrgGrant) for CapDeploy verbs resolves to ReasonDeniedNoCapability (same as the CapRead by-id variant) because the engine's grant filter `g.Scope.OrganizationID != principal.OrganizationID` skips the foreign-org grant entirely BEFORE any capability check. The verdict is identical to BE-0276's foreign-home-org pin — the only thing that changes between CapRead and CapDeploy at this guard is the docstring framing (mutating-side-effect-at-Dokploy vs. cross-tenant-read-of-provisioning-history). The mechanical lesson: the foreign-home-org grant test is verbatim-clone-friendly across CapRead and CapDeploy bare-{id} variants; only the action constant, fixture id, and principal id need swapping.
ralph/progress.txt:19586:  - The destructive-verb framing for CapDeploy job verbs differs from the CapDeploy preview verbs (preview.delete BE-0270) in semantics, not structure: job.retry's "destructive side-effect" is re-issuing a mutating Dokploy provisioning call against the row's owning resource (replaying a desired-state change), NOT removing a Yalla-owned row. The docstring framing therefore emphasises "re-issue mutating Dokploy work", "replay a destructive desired-state change", "unrecoverable side-effect at the upstream provisioner that no in-Yalla rollback can undo" — distinct from preview.delete's "tear down customer-data row, data-loss incident, unrecoverable without backup". Substitution rule: replace data-loss-incident framing with side-effect-at-provisioner framing for any future job.cancel / job.retry / deployment.cancel-by-id / deployment.rollback-by-id pin. The destructive-side-effect framing is now a reusable substitution candidate for any future CapDeploy bare-{id} verb where the side-effect happens at the Dokploy provisioner, not at the Yalla store.
